

Note: The web version of this game can be played by typing commands at the
prompt, or by clicking on highlighted words. It is possible to reach a good
ending using either method, but some typing is required to unlock every part of
the narrative.

For an authentic experience, please print out a double-sided copy of the
enclosed score extract, and make notes with a soft pencil.

Before we begin, a matter of preference: You are Nevada Elmsbee, artistic
director of the Bournebrook Rill Community Orchestra, and you have a big
performance tonight. Do you wear a suit or a dress?

> suit

Suit it is.

Pas De Deux
An Interactive Performance by Linus Åkesson.
Release 2. Serial number 191125.
Dialog compiler version 0h/04. Library version 0.31.

Type ABOUT for credits.

* * *

The applause begins the minute you enter the hall.

With practiced confidence, you stride into the spotlight and smile sincerely as
you look out over the crowd. It's a good turnout tonight.

Concert hall (facing the audience)
You are standing at the focal point of the grand stage of the Bournebrook Rill
Concert Hall.

This spacious auditorium was built in the seventies, to endorse and promote
local drama productions and musical performances. The interior is pine wood and
concrete, and the ceiling is covered in brown felt panels mounted at odd angles
to improve the acoustics. No windows, of course.

You shake hands with the concertmaster. She sits down, and behind your back the
rest of the orchestra follows her example.

The audience is still clapping wildly.

> audience
It's a full house. Press, politicians, and the local upper crust, mixed with the
usual coterie of friends and relatives of orchestra members. In a unique
collaboration with the town council, tonight's concert is a fundraiser for a new
“mallard habitat” in the Botanic Garden, and you suspect that a large proportion
of the crowd are here primarily to wallow in their own benevolence.

Not that any of that matters, of course. The music does. The music and the
orchestra.

The lights dim, and the applause comes to an end. You hear a few coughs and
excited whispers.

> orchestra
(first turning to face the orchestra)

The Bournebrook Rill Community Orchestra is a kaleidoscopic bunch of happy
amateurs. The musicians are seated in a large semi-circle around the conductor's
stand.

When you moved to the neighbourhood, a complete stranger to everyone, you wanted
to find a way into the local music life. A small sinfonietta happened to be
looking for a new conductor, and you jumped at the opportunity. The ensemble was
in a pretty sorry state back then, but with your experience and their
dedication, things have finally turned around. Your little group is now
attracting some of the best amateur instrumentalists.

The best amateur instrumentalists of Bournebrook Rill, that is.

Expectation is in the air.

The second harp is waiting for your signal.

> semi-circle
The seating arrangement is a semi-circle, roughly made up of four separate
slices.

[A diagram is included in your Pas De Deux package.]

Starting at the far left, you've got the first violins up close, headed by the
concertmaster. Behind them are the two harps, and at the back you can see the
cymbalist and the timpani player.

The second slice begins with the second violins. Behind them are the flutes and
piccolo, then the clarinets and bass clarinet, and at the far back the French
horns.

In the third slice, you've got the violas up front, then the oboes, the English
horn, and the bassoons. Back against the wall are the trumpets, trombones, bass
trombone, and tuba.

And to your right, in the fourth slice, the celli are seated in front of the
bassi.

The second harp is waiting for your signal.

> second harp
Rebecca Ward, second harp, is in her mid-twenties. She has her brown hair tied
in a ponytail.

The town council liked the idea of hosting a charity event for the Botanic
Garden so much that, in addition to the venue, they insisted on throwing in a
bit of cash. This you promptly spent on hiring two professional harpists for the
evening: Joy Patterson, who is teaching at the conservatory, and Rebecca Ward,
one of her top students at the moment.

Rebecca acknowledges your cue, and plays the arpeggiated chord that leads up to
the first bar of the piece.

As though it had a mind of its own, your right hand picks up the baton and
begins to trace out beats in the air.

Andante maestoso
The second harp is playing a series of soft, arpeggiated chords, enhanced by
pizzicatos in the violins, violas, and bassi.

The first harp plucks the individual chord notes, up and down in soothing waves.

> score
A facsimile edition of the relevant pages from the Broude Brothers reprint of
the original 1892 Nutcracker score. The composer's name is displayed prominently
on the cover, lost in transliteration: Peter Ilich Tchaikovsky.

[This is the printed score from your Pas De Deux package.]

