No. 14. PAS DE DEUX

This piece calls for two flutes, a piccolo, two oboes, English horn, two
clarinets, bass clarinet, two bassoons, four French horns, two trumpets, two
trombones, bass trombone, tuba, timpani, cymbals, two harps, and a full string
section.

The first eight bars are visible, two on the left-hand page and six on the
right-hand page. They are mostly made up of a regular accompaniment in the harps
and strings, initially set in motion by the second harp.

The main motif is introduced in the celli, fortissimo, in the second bar on the
right-hand page. You will have to cue this, of course.

You could look at the next page, turn to a given page number, or close the
score.

The second harp continues to play a series of soft, arpeggiated chords, enhanced
by pizzicatos in the violins, violas, and bassi.

The first harp plucks the individual chord notes, up and down in soothing waves.

A glance at the score confirms that you're currently listening to the first bar
on the right-hand page.

> celli
All nine members of the cello section are present, including Harold, the section
leader.

Your timely look acts like a reassuring prompt for Harold and the other
cellists.

They pour out their emotions in a sudden, gripping fortissimo. This is the main
motif of the piece, beginning with a descending major scale on the downbeat,
molto espressivo.

Meanwhile, the harps, violins, violas, and bassi continue their accompaniment,
steadily, reliably.

A glance at the score confirms that you're currently hearing the second bar on
the right-hand page.

