You take a moment to enjoy the silence.

Suddenly, applause, like a whip across your neck. “Bravo!” cries some
particularly annoying member of the audience.

> audience
You turn to face the audience.

Rows upon rows of beaming, clapping people.

You smile genially.

It's a full house. Press, politicians, and the local upper crust, mixed with the
usual coterie of friends and relatives of orchestra members.

A roaring applause fills the concert hall.

The concertmaster stands up, and the rest of the orchestra follows her example.

> applause
The sound of applause is indistinguishable from that of frying pork.

Eventually, the applause dwindles, and the audience settles down, eager for the
next piece. You turn once more to the orchestra.

The rest of the concert proceeds in much the same way as the opening piece. The
audience remains exuberant, and you feel rather invigorated by their puppy-like
enthusiasm.

Encores, flowers. The doors open, and the concert hall is gradually vacated.
Spectators and musicians congregate in small groups that drift into the night.
As usual, you end up personally clearing the stage of chairs and stands before
driving the long way home.

You sleep until early afternoon the next day.

* * *

Kitchen
Breakfast is English muffins with ham and eggs, a dab of hollandaise, and a cup
of steaming hot Darjeeling.

The Herald lies unopened in front of you.

> herald
You snatch the Bournebrook Rill Herald off the table, and eagerly turn to the
culture pages.


Organizers of charity events always run the risk of delivering such astounding
performances that they, rather than the paying spectators, are remembered as the
main benefactors. During last night's concert for the benefit of the Botanic
Garden, maestro Nevada Elmsbee fell into precisely this trap. Technically
immaculate and musically deep, the performance reduced the members of the
audience to mere consumers, getting their money's worth of entertainment for the
evening. They will be all but forgotten by future visitors to the Garden, who,
as they come upon the new Mallard Habitat, will be admiring and rejoicing at the
splashing, quacking, lasting legacy of the Bournebrook Rill Community Orchestra.
— Ignatius E. Putterson, D.Mus.A.


*** Five batons out of five ***

A non-diegetic suggestion: There are many online videos of orchestras performing
Tchaikovsky's Pas De Deux. If you haven't already done so, please go ahead and
watch one now, to get a glimpse of yourself in action.

Would you like to:
     UNDO the last move,
     RESTORE a saved position,
     read a NOTE by the author,
     QUIT the program,
     or RESTART from the beginning?
> note

Thank you for playing my game!

In a Pas De Deux (steps for two), a pair of dancers, usually a male and a
female, perform ballet steps together. A similar kind of timing-critical
interplay exists between conductor and orchestra, and between a work of
interactive fiction and its reader.

The role of the conductor has of course been greatly simplified in the story, to
improve gameplay. While it is true, in a sense, that it's a good idea to move
and behave just as during rehearsals, a real conductor will also use their body
language to communicate and tweak dynamics and timing in response to what the
musicians are doing.

But knowing when and where to look is crucial. And you cannot observe without
being observed.

Linus Åkesson

